  "note": "First election of the urban district council, nine seats for the whole town. Twelve candidates were nominated (the nominations add Thos. Graham and James Wallace, who are not in the result); \"the election will take place on Monday, January 16th\". Polling in the Courthouse; Samuel Sterling, town clerk, returning officer, and William H. Atkinson, solicitor, assessor; the result was announced at a quarter past nine. \"The first nine were accordingly declared duly elected, the defeated candidate being Mr Barr.\" The paper prints Gracey's vote as 55, out of order between 105 and 90 (perhaps 95); O'Hare's 89 has a damaged second digit. The nominations call Gracey John Francis Gracey. No party labels are printed. In September 1899 the council resolved to petition the Local Government Board to change \"the urban elections from the annual to the triennial system\" (Banbridge Chronicle, 9 September 1899, p. 3); by 1905 all the members \"retain office for three years and go out simultaneously\"."
}
